WEBVTT

1
00:00:08.982 --> 00:00:17.948
OLIVIA: Hi, Little Fox readers. This week our person in the news is Maria Sharapova, one of

2
00:00:17.949 --> 00:00:21.696
the top-ranked tennis players in the world. What can you tell us about this fantastic athlete, Ben? BEN:

3
00:00:21.697 --> 00:00:30.709
Well, Olivia, Maria was born in Siberia, Russia, on April 19, 1987, to two very athletic parents.

4
00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:36.865
Her father, Yuri, was an avid tennis player, and Maria loved to watch him play. Maria's parents

5
00:00:36.866 --> 00:00:44.118
didn't have enough money to buy her a racket, but when Maria was four, a family friend gave

6
00:00:44.119 --> 00:00:48.670
her one. It was a large adult racket, so Yuri cut off part of the handle. After

7
00:00:48.671 --> 00:00:58.768
that, Maria spent endless hours practicing—on indoor tennis courts as well as outside in the cold, hitting

8
00:00:58.769 --> 00:01:03.510
balls against the side of her house. Her father pushed her to practice constantly, even when she didn't

9
00:01:03.511 --> 00:01:10.915
want to. By the time she was six, she had become so good that her parents decided

10
00:01:10.916 --> 00:01:19.735
to take her to a tennis clinic in Moscow. People at the clinic were very impressed with Maria

11
00:01:19.736 --> 00:01:25.233
and told her parents about a training academy in the United States called the Bollettieri Tennis Academy.

12
00:01:25.234 --> 00:01:30.063
It was a very prestigious school, where many world tennis champions had trained. For a full year

13
00:01:30.064 --> 00:01:34.646
Maria's parents saved money to travel to America. Sadly, though, only Maria and her father were able to

14
00:01:34.647 --> 00:01:41.638
get visas. Maria was just seven years old when she left Russia, and two years would pass

15
00:01:41.639 --> 00:01:48.216
before she saw her mother again. Maria and her father arrived in the U.S. with only seven

16
00:01:48.217 --> 00:01:53.142
hundred dollars, and they spoke no English. Then, at the Bollettieri Academy, they received terrible news. Maria was

17
00:01:53.143 --> 00:01:59.793
too young for the school—she would have to wait two years, until she was nine, to try

18
00:01:59.794 --> 00:02:05.632
out! OLIVIA: That must have been really upsetting for Maria and her dad. What did they do?

19
00:02:05.633 --> 00:02:10.971
BEN: They decided to stay in the U.S. until Maria was old enough to try out for the

20
00:02:10.972 --> 00:02:19.192
Academy. In the meantime Yuri spent a lot of time coaching Maria, and she started competing in—and

21
00:02:19.193 --> 00:02:24.112
winning—some small local tournaments. When Maria was nine years old, she got an invitation to try out

22
00:02:24.113 --> 00:02:29.760
for the Academy. And she did so well, the school offered her a full scholarship. That was a

23
00:02:29.761 --> 00:02:37.268
good thing, Olivia, because tuition there was about $46,000 a year—a lot of money! Life at the

24
00:02:37.269 --> 00:02:41.171
Bollettieri Academy was not easy for Maria. It was a boarding school, which meant she lived there

25
00:02:41.172 --> 00:02:45.318
full-time. She shared a room with three 16-year-old girls, and they teased her for being quiet and not

26
00:02:45.319 --> 00:02:52.165
speaking English. Between the bullying, and the competitive atmosphere at the school, Maria had to learn to

27
00:02:52.166 --> 00:02:59.149
be self-reliant and tough. In addition to her regular schoolwork, Maria was expected to practice tennis about

28
00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:05.728
six hours a day. When she was 14, she started competing professionally, and during the next two years

29
00:03:05.729 --> 00:03:13.227
she won some minor tournaments. Maria impressed tennis fans with her aggressive playing style and her ability

30
00:03:13.228 --> 00:03:21.315
to hit the ball hard—she had a very powerful serve. At 1.8 meters tall, her height allowed her

31
00:03:21.316 --> 00:03:27.799
to reach shots much more easily than most female players. In addition, Maria stood out because she

32
00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:33.883
had a tendency to shriek and grunt as she hit the ball. In fact, Olivia, Maria's grunts

33
00:03:33.884 --> 00:03:42.623
have actually reached 105 decibels, which made her one of the loudest tennis players in the world! OLIVIA:

34
00:03:42.624 --> 00:03:45.874
So when did Maria become famous? BEN: Maria became internationally famous at the age of 17, when

35
00:03:45.875 --> 00:03:53.858
she won the 2004 Wimbledon tournament in London, England. Wimbledon is one of the four Grand Slam

36
00:03:53.859 --> 00:04:01.120
tennis tournaments held each year, so this was a really big deal. By 2005 Maria was ranked the

37
00:04:01.121 --> 00:04:10.408
number one women's tennis player in the world, and she had lucrative deals with numerous companies to

38
00:04:10.409 --> 00:04:16.747
endorse their products: cameras, watches, drinks, tennis rackets, cell phones, handbags, jewelry, shoes, clothing—you name it. She

39
00:04:16.748 --> 00:04:21.831
was on the covers of magazines, in TV commercials, and on billboards. By the following year, Maria was

40
00:04:21.832 --> 00:04:28.323
the highest-paid female athlete anywhere in the world. Unfortunately Maria has suffered a few injuries since she

41
00:04:28.324 --> 00:04:34.416
started competing professionally. In 2006 she bruised her ankle bone, causing her to miss some important tournaments.

42
00:04:34.417 --> 00:04:40.423
Then she developed bursitis in her shoulder, which is an inflammation of the muscles. Swinging the racket was

43
00:04:40.424 --> 00:04:45.579
difficult, and Maria finally had to have surgery in 2008. After that she took several months off

44
00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:51.568
from tennis to let her shoulder heal. Since then she has started competing again, and even though

45
00:04:51.569 --> 00:04:56.225
her shoulder isn't as strong as it once was, she still ranks very high. OLIVIA: Maria is also

46
00:04:56.226 --> 00:05:04.544
known for being pretty fashionable on the court, right? BEN: That's right, Olivia. Maria loves fashion, and

47
00:05:04.545 --> 00:05:09.057
she helps design all of her competition outfits. Many of them are planned at least a year

48
00:05:09.058 --> 00:05:15.888
in advance so that they can be sold in stores around the same time that Maria is wearing

49
00:05:15.889 --> 00:05:23.292
them. When designing, she takes into consideration the color of the court she'll be playing on, the

50
00:05:23.293 --> 00:05:27.767
time of day she'll likely be playing, and what she thinks will be in style by the time

51
00:05:27.768 --> 00:05:35.107
the competition takes place. Maria has also helped to design shoes, cell phone accessories, sunglasses, and—get this—candy!

52
00:05:35.108 --> 00:05:42.353
Maria is aware that she won't be able to compete as a tennis player forever, so she

53
00:05:42.354 --> 00:05:50.012
is determined to set up other business plans now. This way she won't ever have to worry about

54
00:05:50.013 --> 00:05:54.089
earning a living. She has also served as a Goodwill Ambassador for the United Nations, becoming involved

55
00:05:54.090 --> 00:06:05.417
in the development and recovery of the area surrounding Chernobyl where a nuclear accident occurred in 1986.

56
00:06:05.418 --> 00:06:10.908
OLIVIA: Wow, it sounds like Maria is as busy off the court as she is on the court.

57
00:06:10.909 --> 00:06:19.960
Well, that's it for this edition of People in the News . We'll see you next week!

